// ============================================================================
// Resolution · STRUCTURAL SATISFACTION  (the edge that exists in no syntax)
//
// TypeScript is STRUCTURALLY typed, and the schema's measurements say what that
// costs a nominal engine: 60.4% of classes satisfy their interfaces with NO
// `implements` clause, and 21.5% of assignable pairs appear in no syntax anywhere.
// So the `IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE` model that works in Java sees, at best, two fifths
// of the conformance graph here.
//
// ── WHY THIS IS NOT ON THE PRIMARY RESOLUTION PATH ──────────────────────────
// A call on an interface-typed receiver resolves NOMINALLY to the interface's own
// signature, and that is the CORRECT answer against the oracle: `getResolvedSignature`
// names the declaration the compiler selected, not the implementation that will run.
// So satisfaction is not needed to be right about the target — it is needed to
// continue the CHAIN past a signature into a body, and to widen an argument
// compatibility test that a nominal subtype relation would wrongly reject.
//
// Keeping it off the primary path is deliberate. It is the one relation in this
// engine that could FABRICATE rather than over-approximate, because it is name-based
// and does not compare member TYPES; a class with a `read()` that takes different
// arguments would still be counted as satisfying `Readable`. That is acceptable for
// widening a prune-only test and for a labelled reachability fan; it would not be
// acceptable as the answer to "what does this call resolve to".
//
// ── THE SOUNDNESS ARGUMENT, STATED ──────────────────────────────────────────
// Direction: SOURCE is assignable to TARGET when the source has a member for every
// REQUIRED member of the target. Optional members are excluded because an absent
// optional member does not break assignability — `ts_field.isOptional` (col 15) is
// load-bearing here and nowhere else in the engine. This is a NECESSARY condition
// for assignability, not a sufficient one, so the relation is an OVER-approximation
// of the true satisfies-set and every consumer treats it as such.
//
// ── THE COST BOUND ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Unbounded, this is |classes| x |interfaces| x |members|. Two bounds keep it
// linear-ish: a TARGET must be an interface actually used as an annotation
// (satisfaction_target), and a PAIR must already share at least one required member
// name to be considered at all (sat_pair_seed) — which turns the cross product into
// a name-keyed index join.
//
// ── STRATIFICATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Everything here reads LOW relations only — declared_method, declared_field,
// scope_sibling, ancestor_of_merged_group, ref_type_target — and never the in-scope
// member relations, which lookup_scope ties to expr_type. That is what lets
// structural_implementor feed the dispatch cap, whose negation the fan rule performs.
// The counting aggregate is positive and monotone; comparing the two counts is a
// comparison, not a negation.
// ============================================================================

// ── satisfaction_target(TypeHash) — interfaces worth testing against ────────
// An interface that some annotation actually names. An interface nothing is typed by
// cannot be the receiver of any call, so testing conformance to it buys nothing.
satisfaction_target(t) :- ref_type_target(_, _, t),
    interface_type(t).

// ── satisfaction_source(TypeHash) — classes worth testing ───────────────────
// Only instantiable client types: a value of an interface type at runtime is an
// instance of some class, and a library type conforming to a client interface cannot
// be constructed by the client anyway.
satisfaction_source(t) :- type_decl("client", _, _, _, _, _, t),
    instantiable_type(t).

// ── target_required_member(TargetTypeHash, Name) ────────────────────────────
// The members a conforming value MUST have. Optional members are excluded — see the
// header; including them would make almost nothing satisfy anything.
// LOW-STRATUM member reads, not field_in_scope / method_in_scope — see the note on
// member_name_declared in member-lookup.dl. This whole file feeds the dispatch cap,
// which the fan rule negates, so every relation it touches has to sit below the
// resolution fixpoint. Reading the in-scope relations instead made the cap's negation
// cyclic and Souffle named 33 relations in one unstratifiable component.
target_required_member(t, name) :- satisfaction_target(t),
    scope_sibling(t, sib),
    declared_field(sib, name, "false", f),
    field_kind(_, _, _, "false", f).
target_required_member(t, name) :- satisfaction_target(t),
    scope_sibling(t, sib),
    declared_method(sib, name, "false", _).
target_required_member(t, name) :- satisfaction_target(t),
    group_of(t, g),
    ancestor_of_merged_group(_, g, anc),
    scope_sibling(anc, asib),
    declared_method(asib, name, "false", _).

target_required_count(t, n) :- satisfaction_target(t),
    n = count : { target_required_member(t, _) }.

// ── sat_pair_seed(Source, Target) — the pairs worth counting ────────────────
// Sharing one required member name is the cheap precondition. A class that shares
// none cannot satisfy the interface, and this is what stops the relation being a
// cross product.
sat_pair_seed(s, t) :- target_required_member(t, name),
    satisfaction_source(s),
    member_name_declared(s, name).

// ── sat_covered(Source, Target, Name) / sat_cover_count ─────────────────────
sat_covered(s, t, name) :- sat_pair_seed(s, t),
    target_required_member(t, name),
    member_name_declared(s, name).
sat_cover_count(s, t, n) :- sat_pair_seed(s, t),
    n = count : { sat_covered(s, t, _) }.

// ── type_satisfies(SourceTypeHash, TargetTypeHash) ──────────────────────────
// Every required member covered. `k > 0` excludes the empty interface, which
// everything satisfies and which therefore carries no information — admitting it
// would make every class an implementor of every marker interface in the tree.
type_satisfies(s, t) :- sat_cover_count(s, t, k),
    target_required_count(t, k),
    k > 0.

// A nominal implements clause is satisfaction too, and it is the authoritative kind:
// the programmer asserted it and the compiler checked it. Included here so consumers
// have ONE relation to read rather than having to union two.
type_satisfies(s, t) :- type_subtype_star(s, t).

// ── structural_implementor(TargetTypeHash, SourceTypeHash) ──────────────────
// The reachability fan, and the reason to be careful. Restricted to targets with NO
// nominal implementor at all: where the codebase does declare `implements`, that
// declaration is better evidence than a name match, and mixing the two would bury a
// precise answer under a heuristic one.
target_has_nominal_implementor(t) :- implementors(t, _).
structural_implementor(t, s) :- type_satisfies(s, t),
    satisfaction_target(t),
    s != t,
    !target_has_nominal_implementor(t).

// ── satisfaction_unmeasured(TargetTypeHash, Name) ───────────────────────────
// EVERY SUPPRESSION COUNTABLE. A required member of a tested interface that NO
// candidate source has — the reason a satisfies edge was not derived. Reading this
// is how "why is nothing implementing this interface" gets an answer instead of a
// shrug.
member_covered_by_someone(t, name) :- sat_covered(_, t, name).
satisfaction_unmeasured(t, name) :- target_required_member(t, name),
    !member_covered_by_someone(t, name).
